{
  "requirement": "`habeas_sdk::graph` MUST expose the graph's associated types and content lookup operation:\n\n```rust,ignore\npub trait Graph: Send + Sync {\n    /// The *identifier* identifies a particular node in the graph.\n    type NodeId: Clone + Eq + Send + Sync;\n\n    /// The *contents* of a node defines what is given to a validator\n    /// when the node is read.\n    type NodeContent: Send + Sync;\n\n    /// The *label* between edges defines the kind of verification relationship\n    /// between two nodes.\n    type EdgeLabel: Clone + Send + Sync;\n\n    /// User-defined errors that can occur when fetching node content or performing\n    /// other operations.\n    type Error: Send;\n\n    /// Fetch the content of a node.\n    fn node_content(&self, node: &Self::NodeId) -> Result<Self::NodeContent, Self::Error>;\n\n    // ... additional methods defined below ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations.** `requirements.sdk.graph.domain_types` is a type-level contract with these parts:\n- `habeas_sdk::graph::Graph` is public and `Send + Sync`.\n- It has four associated types: `NodeId` (`Clone + Eq + Send + Sync`), `NodeContent` (`Send + Sync`), `EdgeLabel` (`Clone + Send + Sync`) and `Error` (`Send`).\n- It has a content lookup `node_content(&self, &NodeId) -> Result<NodeContent, Error>`.\n\nThe one behavioural part is that `node_content` returns the node's content or a user-defined error.\n\n**How the tests cover this.** The trait in `crates/habeas-sdk/src/graph.rs#trait[Graph]` declares exactly this shape. It also declares `JudgmentMetadata`, `node_path` and `outgoing_edges`, which belong to other requirements.\n\nThe marked tests implement or use `Graph` with several unrelated types, so the signature and the associated types are checked at compile time:\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#fn[default_tool_accepts_scalar_results_and_keeps_the_exact_prefix]` uses a `Memory` graph with `Node` ids. The test checks that the content delivered to the validator is `\"source\"`. It also checks that the dependency fingerprint records that content.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#fn[validation_is_usable_without_any_evidence_storage]` uses the same kind of graph. It checks the result and rationale, and that the dependency records `\"ordinary computation\"`.\n- `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/contracts.rs#fn[checker_needs_no_validation_and_storage_preserves_nonserializable_metadata]` uses a `GraphOnly` graph. It reads content through the checker, so content changes give `Stale`. It also injects `load`, `read` and `edges` failures and asserts that each user-defined `Error` value comes back unchanged. It wraps the storage futures in a `send::<T: Send>` helper, which exercises the `Send` bounds.\n- `src/authoring/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[review_graph_delegates_unprepared_sources_and_paths_to_the_domain]` calls `node_content` on the real `ReviewGraph` and `HabeasDomain`. It asserts the delivered content equals the domain's content and that a missing outline fails with an `Err`.\n- `src/sdk.rs#mod[tests]/fn[requirement_edges_and_input_freshness_use_the_sdk]` and `fn[habeas_paths_preserve_opaque_segments_and_separate_all_node_kinds]` use `HabeasDomain` with its own `NodeId`, `NodeContent`, `EdgeLabel` and `Error` types. They call `node_content` and assert the resulting content and fingerprints.\n\n**Bounds.** The `Send` and `Sync` bounds are checked at compile time. `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/validation.rs` has `assert_send_sync(&ctx)` on a context built from a `Graph`. The `Send`-future helpers named above also cover them. The SDK's generic code relies on `Clone` and `Eq` for `NodeId` and on `Clone` for `EdgeLabel`, so removing those bounds would break the build. The one gap is that no test statically asserts that a weaker or stronger bound is rejected. For a declarative trait signature, I treat that as covered by the compile-time use above.\n\n**Result.** Success (content returned and fingerprinted) and failure (user-defined `Error` propagated or returned as `Err`) are both asserted, across several distinct implementors and type choices. I found no unacknowledged gap.",
  "references": "00a6c109c5ae23ac2c87dc0627d20dcf1d3101a5c1eb749cecc1db614e8e1143",
